+### `scaling-mode`
+
+<sup>Since: next release</sup>
+
+Set how the display hardware scales a [`mode`](#mode) that doesn't match the native resolution of the monitor.
+This sets the `scaling mode` property of the DRM connector, so the scaling is done by the display engine of the GPU at no rendering cost.
+
+- `"full"`: stretch the image to fill the whole monitor, ignoring the aspect ratio.
+- `"full-aspect"`: scale the image as much as possible while preserving the aspect ratio, with black bars.
+- `"center"`: show the image unscaled in the middle of the monitor, with black borders.
+- `"none"`: no preference, the driver decides; for example, amdgpu uses `"full-aspect"` for non-native modes on built-in laptop panels.
+
+When this option is unset, niri doesn't change the property.
+Not all drivers and connectors support it; `niri msg outputs` shows the current scaling mode when the output supports it, and niri will print a warning if it cannot set the configured one.
+
+Niri itself only sees the mode resolution: everything, including the cursor, is rendered at that resolution and then scaled by the hardware, so pointer input keeps matching the picture.
+Screenshots and screencasts are not stretched.
+
+When scaling, some drivers (e.g. amdgpu on laptop panels) keep driving the monitor with its native timings, so the refresh rate of a non-native mode may be ignored.
+Prefer the native refresh rate for custom modes.
+
+```kdl
+// Stretch 4:3 modes to fill a 16:9 laptop panel.
+output "eDP-1" {
+    mode custom=true "1440x1080@240"
+    scaling-mode "full"
+}
+```
+
+You can also change it at runtime, for example from a key binding:
+
+```sh
+niri msg output eDP-1 scaling-mode full
+```
+
 ### `hot-corners`
 
